1. The independence assumption, and where it silently breaks
Claude stated the assumption: "That assumes the layers fail independently, and in practice errors are correlated, so real gains are smaller." This is correct but understated. For T5 the assumption is approximately true because each layer is a different physical system with different physics: a biometric sensor fails differently from a baggage conveyor, which fails differently from a human immigration officer. The defect-quotients are orthogonal because the engineering disciplines are orthogonal.

For an AI verification stack, the layers are not orthogonal. Consider the canonical stack:

Base model generates an answer.
A second model (or the same model in a critic role) checks it.
A retrieval system grounds it against a corpus.
A formal verifier (Lean, Isabelle) checks the logical skeleton.
A human reviewer signs off.
Claude's multiplication — 10% × 10% × 10% ≈ 0.1% — assumes these layers fail independently. But they do not, and the failure of independence is the load-bearing fact:

Layers 1 and 2 share a training corpus. The critic model was trained on the same internet the generator was. If the generator's quotient misfires on a fact because the corpus is silent or wrong about it, the critic — having the same blind spot — will confirm the error. This is Royen at the layer boundary: the community's blind spot propagates as the verifier's blind spot.
Layers 1, 2, and 3 share a form-of-life. The retrieval system retrieves what the corpus considers relevant, which is what the community considers relevant, which is the same form-of-life that trained the model. A Royen-style claim that the corpus has not yet admitted will not be retrieved, will not be confirmed, will not be grounded. The three layers conspire because they share one quotient.
Layer 4 is genuinely orthogonal — when it applies. Formal verification is the one layer whose defect-quotient is independent of the corpus, because Lean's kernel checks syntax and inference rules, not social consensus. This is why AI math systems pair generation with formal checkers: it is the only layer in the stack that breaks the correlation. But it only applies where the claim is formalizable. For natural-language claims, moral claims, historical claims, there is no Lean, and the orthogonality vanishes.
Layer 5 is the community itself. The human reviewer is the form-of-life the model was trained on. The reviewer's blind spots are the model's blind spots, by construction.
So the multiplication Claude describes is, for most AI applications, a multiplication of correlated failures, not independent ones. The true gain is not 
