3. What fraction of LLM errors are actually systematic?
This is the empirical question on which the entire lemma turns, and the evidence is not encouraging for the 6σ claim:

Factual recall errors are largely systematic. If Wikipedia, the common training corpus, is silent or wrong about fact X, all 13 LLMs will be wrong about X in correlated ways. They may hallucinate different wrong answers (which looks like independence but isn't — they are all drawing from the same absence). The Royen case is the canonical example: every model trained on the post-2014 mathematical corpus would eventually recognize the proof, but every model trained on the pre-recognition corpus would dismiss it, together, for the same reasons.

Reasoning errors are partially systematic. Different architectures have different inductive biases, so genuinely different models (Claude, GPT, Gemini, DeepSeek) will fail on different problems. This is real independence — but it is partial. They share transformer architecture, they share RLHF from similar human communities, they share the same form-of-life. The correlation is less than 1 but well above 0.

Format and style errors are mostly noise. This is where the ensemble genuinely shines, and it is the grain of truth in the enterprise claim. If the task is "grade this essay on a rubric," much of the variance between agents is sampling noise, and 13 agents will converge reliably. The Google AI result's "deconstructed rubrics" — converting subjective grading to yes/no checklist questions — is precisely the move that converts systematic error (subjective judgment) into noise (factual tracking), making the ensemble effective.

