4. The rubric decomposition is real progress — but it has a boundary
The Google AI result's "Deconstructed Rubrics" deserves credit. It is the most operationally important idea in the whole proposal. Breaking "grade this essay" into "Is fact X present? Yes/No" converts a fuzzy quotient into a sharp one. Sharp quotients are more likely to be independently verifiable, because the error becomes "did the model correctly detect fact X" rather than "did the model correctly apply a standard." The former is a detection problem with measurable systematic/noise structure; the latter is a judgment problem dominated by shared form-of-life.

But the decomposition has a boundary, and the boundary is exactly the Royen line. Consider: "Is this proof correct? Yes/No." This is a deconstructed rubric question. It is sharp. But the systematic error on this question — for any LLM trained on the mathematical community's corpus — includes the entire Royen blind spot. All 13 LLMs, asked "is this Word-document proof by a retired statistician correct?", will inherit the community's reception quotient. They will all lean toward "probably not, because it doesn't look like a real proof." The decomposition does not help, because the systematic error is in the yes/no question itself, not in the aggregation.

Decomposition helps when the sub-questions are cleaner than the parent question. It does not help when the parent question's difficulty is inherited by every sub-question. Mathematical correctness, moral permissibility, historical significance — these are questions where the difficulty is inherited, and no amount of rubric decomposition will make them independent across agents that share a training corpus.

