The two-level structure (13 LLMs × 13 agents each) is better than a flat 169-agent panel, because it models the actual correlation structure: within-LLM correlation is high (same weights, same training), across-LLM correlation is moderate (different models, overlapping corpus). The internal vote strips within-LLM noise; the across-LLM vote strips some systematic error that varies by architecture.

But the systematic floor survives. If the systematic component is 3% — meaning 3% of questions are ones where every model in the ensemble fails together because they share the blind spot — then the 13×13 system stays at 3% = 30,000 DPMO = 3.3σ. The two-level structure buys you maybe 0.2σ of improvement over a flat panel, by being more efficient with the noise component. It does not touch the floor.

