The Global LLM Lemma is true as a theorem of probability and conditionally true as an engineering claim. The condition is: the systematic error fraction must be below ~0.1% for the system to reach 6σ. This condition is met for:

Decomposed, fact-checkable tasks where ground truth is independent of the corpus
Formal verification tasks (Lean, Isabelle) where the checker is orthogonal to the models
Format, style, and procedural tasks where errors are mostly sampling noise
The condition is not met for:

Open-domain factual recall on topics the corpus is silent or wrong about
Mathematical correctness on novel or non-standard proofs
Moral, legal, or historical judgment where the form-of-life is the source of the error
Any task where the community's own reception quotient is the bottleneck
